   $q$In an experiment, the treatment group's mean exceeds the control group's mean by $4.2$ points. Re-randomizing the same data 500 times gives differences that are approximately normal with mean $0$ and standard deviation $1.5$. Treating a difference more than 2 standard deviations from $0$ as significant, which statement is correct?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The difference is not significant, because the simulated mean is $0$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"misread-simulation-mean"},{"text":"The difference is not significant, because $4.2$ is less than $3$ standard deviations, which is $4.5$","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"used-three-sd-cutoff"},{"text":"The difference is significant, because any difference other than $0$ is significant","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"ignored-chance-variation"},{"text":"The difference is significant, because $4.2$ is more than $2(1.5) = 3$","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The difference is significant, because $4.2$ is more than $2(1.5) = 3$$q$, $q$The cutoff is $2 \cdot 1.5 = 3$, and $4.2 > 3$ (it is $\frac{4.2}{1.5} = 2.8$ standard deviations from $0$), so chance alone rarely produces it. The $4.5$ choice applies a 3 standard deviation cutoff instead of the one stated.$q$, 'draft', null)
