First, bike lanes make riders safer. When a lane is painted, drivers know where to expect bikes. The town of Elmford painted bike lanes three years ago. Since then, Elmford has had fewer crashes between cars and bikes.

Second, bike lanes would help more kids ride to school. Last spring, our class asked 60 students how they get to school. Half of them said they would ride a bike if the street felt safer.

Third, more bikes would mean fewer cars. Fewer cars would mean less traffic in front of the school each morning. It would also mean cleaner air near the playground.

Maple Street is also home to a busy bakery that sells fresh bread every morning.

Some people worry that bike lanes cost a lot. But paint costs much less than building a new road. The lanes would last for many years.

Bike lanes are a small change that would make a big difference. Let's paint Maple Street.$q$, 'draft')
