-- Passage rg4-argument-evidence-p03
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('020df814-4c89-5f82-b161-6d50b43d7cdf', 'rg4-argument-evidence-p03', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Barn Owls on the Farm$q$, $q$Farmers have many problems, and mice are one of the biggest. These small animals eat grain and chew holes in bags of seed. Some farmers have found a helper that works for free: the barn owl.

Barn owls are excellent hunters. They hunt at night, when mice are most active. Their hearing is so sharp that they can find a mouse moving under grass in total darkness.

A family of barn owls eats a great many mice. One adult owl can eat three or four mice every night. A family with young owlets may catch more than 3,000 mice in a single year. That is a lot of grain saved.

Owls are also safer for the farm than poison. Poison can harm pets and other wild animals by mistake. Owls hunt only small animals like mice and voles.

Farmers who want owls can build wooden nest boxes and hang them on poles or barns. Farmer Tomas Reyes put up ten boxes on his land. Within two years, owl families lived in eight of them. He also saw far fewer mice in his fields.

Barn owls also have pale, heart-shaped faces that many people find beautiful.

For a farmer with a mouse problem, a barn owl can be a best friend.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
where public.passages.status = 'draft';
