-- Passage rg4-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('1f0accbe-cc6d-570e-80b4-2f8cd4318cee', 'rg4-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g4-argument-evidence'), null,
        '3-5', $q$Why Some Cities Paint Roofs White$q$, $q$On a sunny summer day, a dark roof can get hot enough to burn your hand. Some cities now ask builders to paint roofs white or another light color. There are good reasons for this idea.

Light colors reflect sunlight, while dark colors soak it up. You can test this yourself. Place a white sheet of paper and a black sheet of paper in the sun. After an hour, the black paper will feel much warmer.

A cooler roof means a cooler building. In one test, scientists painted half of a warehouse roof white and left the other half black. On hot afternoons, the rooms under the white half stayed several degrees cooler.

Cooler buildings need less air conditioning. Air conditioners use a lot of electricity. When a building stays cooler on its own, the owner pays a smaller electric bill.

White roofs can help a whole city, too. Cities with many dark roofs and roads trap heat, so they stay hot even at night. If many roofs were light colored, neighborhoods could cool off faster after sunset.

Of course, white roofs are not perfect. They can get dirty and may need to be cleaned or painted again every few years. Still, many cities think the benefits are worth the work.$q$, 'draft')
